window.__labLoaded({"id":"3.5","title":"Normal Approximation to the Binomial","goal":"Use the airline that books 200 people (10% no-shows) to see how well a normal curve approximates the exact binomial answer for fewer than 15 no-shows.","question":"An airline sells 200 tickets on a flight and about 10% of ticket holders do not show up. The manager asks: what is the chance that fewer than 15 passengers fail to show? The exact answer needs the binomial distribution, but a normal curve is much easier to use. How close does the normal approximation get, and does the half-unit continuity correction help?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Check the condition","text":"The normal curve is a good stand-in for the binomial only when both np and n(1 - p) are at least 10. Here n = 200 and p = 0.1, so we check both numbers first.","code":"n, p = 200, 0.1\n\nprint('np      =', n * p)\nprint('n(1-p)  =', n * (1 - p))\nprint('safe to approximate?', n * p >= 10 and n * (1 - p) >= 10)","explain":["Store the number of tickets and the no-show probability.","Print n x p, the expected number of no-shows.","Print n x (1 - p), the expected number of shows.","Check the rule of thumb: both must be at least 10 for the normal curve to be a good stand-in."],"meaning":"The expected no-shows are 20 and the expected shows 180, both well above 10, so the check prints True. The normal approximation is safe to use here.","out":"np      = 20.0\nn(1-p)  = 180.0\nsafe to approximate? True","figs":[]},{"title":"The exact binomial answer","text":"Fewer than 15 no-shows means 14 or fewer. The binomial cdf adds up all fifteen terms for us, so this is the true value we want the normal curve to match.","code":"from scipy import stats\n\nexact = stats.binom.cdf(14, n, p)\n\nprint(f'exact P(X <= 14) = {exact:.4f}')","explain":["Load the statistics tools from SciPy.","Fewer than 15 means 14 or fewer, so ask the binomial cdf at 14. This is the exact answer.","Print the exact probability."],"meaning":"The exact chance of fewer than 15 no-shows is 0.0929, about 9.3%. This is the true value our approximation must match.","out":"exact P(X <= 14) = 0.0929","figs":[]},{"title":"The normal approximation","text":"Match the normal curve to the binomial's mean np and SD sqrt(np(1 - p)). The continuity correction uses 14.5 because the bar for 14 stretches from 13.5 to 14.5.","code":"from math import sqrt\n\nmu = n * p\nsigma = sqrt(n * p * (1 - p))\n\nwith_cc = stats.norm.cdf(14.5, mu, sigma)\nwithout = stats.norm.cdf(14, mu, sigma)\n\nprint(f'mu = {mu:.1f}, sigma = {sigma:.3f}')\nprint(f'normal with correction    = {with_cc:.4f}')\nprint(f'normal without correction = {without:.4f}')","explain":["Load the square root function.","The matching normal curve has the binomial's mean n x p.","It has the binomial's SD, the square root of n x p x (1 - p).","Apply the continuity correction: use 14.5, the edge of the bar for 14, when using the normal.","Without the correction: use 14, the middle of the bar.","Print the mean and SD of the matching normal.","Print the answer with the correction.","Print the answer without the correction."],"meaning":"The matching normal has mean 20.0 and SD 4.243. With the correction it gives 0.0974, without it 0.0786, compared with the exact 0.0929. The corrected value is clearly the closer.","out":"mu = 20.0, sigma = 4.243\nnormal with correction    = 0.0974\nnormal without correction = 0.0786","figs":[]},{"title":"How close is it?","text":"The error with the half-unit correction is far smaller than without it. That is why the correction matters, even for a big n.","code":"print(f'error with correction    = {abs(with_cc - exact):.4f}')\nprint(f'error without correction = {abs(without - exact):.4f}')","explain":["Print how far the corrected answer is from the exact one.","Print how far the uncorrected answer is."],"meaning":"The error with the correction is only 0.0045, but without it the error is 0.0143, about three times bigger. The half-unit shift is worth doing even with a big n.","out":"error with correction    = 0.0045\nerror without correction = 0.0143","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"n <- 200; p <- 0.1\npbinom(14, n, p)\npnorm(14.5, mean = n * p, sd = sqrt(n * p * (1 - p)))\npnorm(14,   mean = n * p, sd = sqrt(n * p * (1 - p)))","explain":["Store the number of tickets and the no-show probability.","The exact answer: the binomial cdf at 14.","The normal approximation with the continuity correction (14.5).","The normal approximation without the correction (14)."],"expect":"pbinom: 0.0929 (exact); pnorm with 14.5: 0.0974; pnorm with 14: 0.0786","read":"The three numbers are 0.0929 (exact), 0.0974 (normal with 14.5) and 0.0786 (normal with 14). The corrected one is closer to the exact answer.","note":"pbinom is the exact answer. The two pnorm lines are the approximation with and without the continuity correction."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use CDF.BINOM and CDF.NORMAL)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE exact = CDF.BINOM(14, 200, 0.1).\nCOMPUTE mu = 200 * 0.1.\nCOMPUTE sigma = SQRT(200 * 0.1 * 0.9).\nCOMPUTE with_cc = CDF.NORMAL(14.5, mu, sigma).\nCOMPUTE without_cc = CDF.NORMAL(14, mu, sigma).\nFORMATS exact with_cc without_cc (F8.4).\nLIST exact with_cc without_cc.","explain":["Make a tiny data set so there is one row to compute in.","Start the data block.","One row containing the number 1.","End the data block.","The exact answer from the binomial distribution.","The mean of the matching normal.","The SD of the matching normal.","Normal approximation with the continuity correction.","Normal approximation without the correction.","Show four decimals.","Print the three answers."],"read":"LIST shows exact = 0.0929, with_cc = 0.0974 and without_cc = 0.0786. The corrected normal is closer to the exact answer, as in Python.","note":"CDF.BINOM(k, n, p) is the exact answer; CDF.NORMAL(value, mean, sd) is the approximation."},"stata":{"code":"display binomial(200, 14, 0.1)\ndisplay normal((14.5 - 20) / sqrt(200 * 0.1 * 0.9))\ndisplay normal((14 - 20) / sqrt(200 * 0.1 * 0.9))","explain":["Exact answer: the binomial cdf at 14 for 200 tickets and 10% no-shows.","Normal approximation with the correction: standardise 14.5 by hand with (x - mean) / SD, then use normal().","Normal approximation without the correction, standardising 14."],"read":"The three lines print .0929, .0974 and .0786 (rounded). The first is exact and the other two are approximations, and the corrected one is closer.","note":"binomial(n,k,p) is the exact P(X <= k). The two normal() lines standardise the cut-off by hand, with and without the half-unit correction."},"conclusion":"The chance that fewer than 15 passengers fail to show is 0.0929 exactly. The normal approximation gives 0.0974 with the continuity correction and 0.0786 without it, so the correction cuts the error from 0.0143 to 0.0045. The caution: the approximation is only trustworthy when np and n(1 - p) are both at least 10.","practice":{"scenario":"A pollster surveys 500 voters. Past polls show that 40% support a certain measure. She wants to know how likely it is that 185 or fewer in the sample support it, and whether the normal approximation to the binomial gets this right.","data":"n = 500 voters, p = 0.4. Question: P(X <= 185). Compare the exact binomial answer with the normal approximation with and without the continuity correction.","tasks":["Check that np and n(1 - p) are both at least 10 and find the SD of the matching normal.","Find the exact P(X <= 185).","Find the normal approximation with 185.5 and with 185.","Which version is closer, and by how much?"],"hint":"The matching normal has mean n x p and SD sqrt(n x p x (1 - p)). Use binom.cdf for the exact answer and norm.cdf(185.5, mu, sigma) for the corrected version.","solution":{"code":"from math import sqrt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nn, p = 500, 0.4\nmu, sigma = n * p, sqrt(n * p * (1 - p))\nprint(f'np = {mu}, n(1-p) = {n * (1 - p)}, sigma = {sigma:.3f}')\n\nexact = stats.binom.cdf(185, n, p)\nwith_cc = stats.norm.cdf(185.5, mu, sigma)\nwithout = stats.norm.cdf(185, mu, sigma)\nprint(f'exact = {exact:.4f}, with correction = {with_cc:.4f}, without = {without:.4f}')\nprint(f'errors: {abs(with_cc - exact):.4f} and {abs(without - exact):.4f}')","answer":"np = 200 and n(1 - p) = 300, both far above 10, and the matching SD is 10.954. The exact P(X <= 185) is 0.0924. The normal gives 0.0928 with the correction and 0.0855 without. The errors are 0.0004 and 0.0069, so the corrected version is much closer.","out":"np = 200.0, n(1-p) = 300.0, sigma = 10.954\nexact = 0.0924, with correction = 0.0928, without = 0.0855\nerrors: 0.0004 and 0.0069","figs":[]}}});
